shadow of the Tomb Raider caught my eye  with its unique modular difficulty  you're able to customize not only the  combat but the exploration and puzzles  as well to fit the experience you're  looking for games typically don't adjust  things like platforming and puzzles and  their difficulties so I was intrigued  about how this would affect to the  experience so I've cranked up the  settings to max to see how tough shadows  the Tomb Raider can really get I'm  primarily gonna be talking about the  game's Hard difficulty called one with  the jungle there is a difficulty setting  above it which I'll get to at the end of  the video just know that it's the exact  same thing with one major difference for  added challenge I also turned off  survival instincts which is the game's  version of Batman vision there's also no  option to turn off HUD for that truly  hardcore experience but that's okay as  the game's HUD is already very minimal  and there's no mini-map in the game  period let's start with the combat in  addition to increased health and damage  from enemies help regeneration in combat  is removed ammo boxes are more scarce  the hit marker on the reticle is removed  and enemies are no longer highlighted in  survival instincts something that  doesn't really matter since I turned it  off anyways you'll notice that in the  first half of the game there are far  less combat encounters than previous  Tomb Raider games when you do encounter  enemies the game is almost always  encouraging a stealth playthrough and it  certainly becomes much more necessary on  the hardest difficulty but honestly the  combat is never that demanding enemies  are easy to stealth kill either up  closer with your foe you'll go down  quickly if shot at but it's not hard to  escape line-of-sight and most enemies go  down just as quick as you I highly  recommend trying the game on the hardest  combat setting combats fairly easy on  lower difficulties and hard demands a  lot more from the stealth options which  is where I think the combat really  shines with more becoming a savage  predator by the time combat picks up  significantly in the second half of the  game you should be more than  well-prepared for it next up is  exploration the big difference is the  removal of the white paint that lets you  know what is crumble and where to go  next this is the most unique and  interesting of all the difficulty  settings and I was curious how well this  would actually work because there is a  good reason that white paint exists  games like Tomb Raider uncharted or  basically any game with lots of climbing  use color as an in-game way of directing  the player without it players can get  confused or frustrated ruining the  pacing and flow of gameplay  these games are typically designed with  these markers in mind so to remove it  seems like a recipe for frustration to  my surprise though I didn't encounter  much trouble I had to spend a lot more  time stopping and thinking for a moment  to figure out where to go and there were  those moments of oh hey is that ledge I  can grab him oh no no I guess not but  for the most part it was a smooth  experience it's actually pretty  liberating to navigate without any hints  relying instead on my own instincts of  what I think I can grab last is puzzle  difficulty which removes any hints laura  will give you along with all glowing  markers from survival instincts and I  love this so much complex puzzles have  become a staple of the Tomb Raider  franchise and I've always enjoyed taking  a break for a good brain teaser now  these aren't like final area of portal  levels of challenge but I did get stuck  several times and it's fun to try and  figure it out only thing you have to  watch out for is puzzles where someone  comes over the radio to tell you a clue  that's necessary if you happen to not be  paying attention you might be out of  luck okay Jonah what's the final number  for shock shell thanks  okay so I did mention that there is one  final difficulty setting known as deadly  obsession it's the exact same thing as  one with the jungle but checkpoints are  removed and you can only save at base  camps playing on this mode can be a lot  of fun you want to stick to stealth  exclusively and really think before  every jump  one wrong move or a stray bullet is all  it takes to lose an hour of progress  base camps are plentiful enough that as  long as you plan it out you'll be okay  especially during the hub areas if you  die at any point however during the  hour-long story heavy intro where there  are no base camps then be prepared to do  that all over again I found some  frustration at times towards the  controls in certain platforming sections  where I felt I was jumping one way only  for Lara to jump somewhere else and fall  to her death in on a mode like deadly  obsession  this can be frustrating also I should  warn you if you pick deadly obsession  you can't change the difficulty setting  at all so make sure you're committed to  it if that's what you're gonna do deadly  obsession is doable and really rewarding  but I think it's a better fit for a  second playthrough where you feel more  comfortable with the game  and that shadow of the Tomb Raider on  its hardest difficulties overall I  didn't find it to be as much of a  challenge compared to other games so if  you're a seasoned Tomb Raider player I  highly recommend getting one with the  jungle ago you can always change the  difficulty later if it's too much for  you or if you've found certain aspects  appealing like no puzzle hints but are  interested in removing the white markers  you can always mix and match to your  liking but however you end up playing  the game I hope you have fun  [Music]  you
 

